Generating multiple choice questions from an article using Java code requires a carefully designed approach that includes natural language processing and machine learning. Unfortunately, generating high-quality, relevant, and thought-provoking questions for such specific content is currently beyond the scope of simple Java code without relying on advanced deep learning models and extensive libraries.

However, I can provide you with a general framework that you can follow to implement a very basic version of a multiple choice question generator.

1. Read the article and tokenize the sentences.
2. Extract keywords from the sentences.
3. Use the keywords to form questions.
4. Generate multiple choice options by including the correct answer along with similar or related false options.

Here is a very simple Java code example to demonstrate this framework:

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class MultipleChoiceQuestionGenerator {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        String articlePath = "path/to/your/article.txt";

        // 1. Read the article
        List<String> sentences = readArticle(articlePath);

        // 2. Extract keywords from the sentences
        List<String> keywords = extractKeywords(sentences);

        // 3. Use the keywords to form questions
        List<String> questions = generateQuestions(keywords);

        // 4. Generate multiple choice options
        for (String question : questions) {
            List<String> options = generateOptions(question, keywords);
            System.out.println(question);
            for (int i = 0; i < options.size(); i++) {
                System.out.println((char) (65 + i) + ". " + options.get(i));
            }
            System.out.println("\n");
        }
    }

    private static List<String> readArticle(String path) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path));
        List<String> sentences = new ArrayList<>();
        String line;
        while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
            for(String sentence : line.split("(?<=[.!?]\\s)")) {
                sentences.add(sentence.trim());
            }
        }
        br.close();
        return sentences;
    }

    private static List<String> extractKeywords(List<String> sentences) {
        List<String> keywords = new ArrayList<>();

        // Just for demonstration purposes, the first 5 words of each sentence will be considered as "keywords"
        // In a real application, you should replace this with a proper keyword extraction algorithm
        for (String sentence : sentences) {
            String[] words = sentence.split(" ");
            for (int i = 0; i < 5 && i < words.length; i++) {
                keywords.add(words[i]);
            }
        }

        return keywords;
    }

    private static List<String> generateQuestions(List<String> keywords) {
        List<String> questions = new ArrayList<>();

        // Just for demonstration purposes, questions are formed by combining the first 3 keywords
        // In a real application, you should replace this with a proper question generation algorithm
        for (int i = 0; i < keywords.size(); i += 3) {
            if (i + 2 < keywords.size()) {
                questions.add("What is the relationship between " + keywords.get(i) + ", " + keywords.get(i + 1) + ", and " + keywords.get(i + 2) + "?");
            }
        }

        return questions;
    }

    private static List<String> generateOptions(String question, List<String> keywords) {
        List<String> options = new ArrayList<>();

        // Just for demonstration purposes, options are randomly picked from keywords
        // In a real application, you should replace this with a proper option generation algorithm
        // that provides a correct answer along with false alternatives
        options.add(keywords.get((int) (Math.random() * keywords.size())));
        options.add(keywords.get((int) (Math.random() * keywords.size())));
        options.add(keywords.get((int) (Math.random() * keywords.size())));

        return options;
    }
}
```

This code is a very basic example and should not be used for generating actual multiple choice questions in a relevant and thought-provoking context. To create a more sophisticated question generator, you could delve into natural language processing libraries or pre-trained deep learning models, like OpenAI's GPT-3.

For Java, you may consider using libraries like [Stanford CoreNLP](https://stanfordnlp.github.io/CoreNLP/index.html) or [OpenNLP](https://opennlp.apache.org/) for natural language processing to help with keyword extraction, named entity recognition, and question generation. Incorporating these libraries will help you to better analyze the text and generate more engaging multiple-choice questions.

In order to access your printer's web interface and check the network settings using C++, you can use third-party libraries like cURL or Boost.Beast. Here, I will provide you with an example using cURL.

First, download and install the libcurl library for C++ from the official cURL website (https://curl.haxx.se/libcurl/).

Below is a simple example of using libcurl to check the network settings of your printer by accessing its web interface:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <curl/curl.h>

size_t WriteCallback(void *contents, size_t size, size_t nmemb, void *userp)
{
    ((std::string *)userp)->append((char *)contents, size * nmemb);
    return size * nmemb;
}

int main()
{
    CURL *curl;
    CURLcode res;
    std::string readBuffer;

    curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_DEFAULT);
    curl = curl_easy_init();

    if (curl)
    {
        std::string printer_ip = "192.168.1.100";
        std::string printer_url = "http://" + printer_ip; // You can add specific paths to access specific settings if needed.

        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, printer_url.c_str());
        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, WriteCallback);
        curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_WRITEDATA, &readBuffer);

        res = curl_easy_perform(curl);

        if (res != CURLE_OK)
        {
            std::cerr << "curl_easy_perform() failed: " << curl_easy_strerror(res) << std::endl;
        }
        else
        {
            std::cout << "Successfully accessed printer's web interface. Printing the content:\n\n";
            std::cout << readBuffer << std::endl;
        }
        curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
    }

    curl_global_cleanup();

    return 0;
}
```

This code initializes libcurl, sets the printer's IP address and URL, and retrieves the content of the web interface into the `readBuffer` string. It then prints the content to the console. Note that you may need to parse the HTML or XML content to extract specific network settings. You can use libraries like Boost.PropertyTree (for XML/JSON) or htmlcxx (for HTML) to parse the content, extract specific settings, and manipulate them according to your needs.

